{"key":"K01","report_id":"R0068","field":"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability","assertions":[{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"MARL-meta converges to maximum expected rewards with higher accuracy than MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC on 5-layer LeNet and 8-layer AlexNet.","page":10,"locator":"VI-B.1, Fig. 4(a)-(b)","excerpt":"meta-RL converges to the maximum expected reward with higher accuracy than the competitive algorithms (MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC) in the two CNN networks, LeNet and AlexNet.","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Across varying channel bandwidths, MARL-meta closely matches the theoretical sub-optimal LDTP benchmark in latency and transmit power while MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC exhibit higher latency and power usage.","page":11,"locator":"VI-B.2, Fig. 5(a)-(b)","excerpt":"the LDTP approach establishes a theoretical sub-optimal benchmark due to its optimized nature, and our proposed meta-MARL method closely matches this benchmark","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"MARL-meta achieves superior convergence and higher sustained volume of reliably shared data among UAVs compared to MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC.","page":11,"locator":"VI-B.3, Fig. 6(a)","excerpt":"The MARL-Meta algorithm demonstrates superior convergence and sustained reliability compared to traditional MARL approaches (MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, MARL-SAC).","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Across Raspberry Pi 3B+ memory capacities (256 MB, 512 MB, 560 MB), MARL-meta delivers average total latency closest to LDTP and outperforms MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC.","page":12,"locator":"VI-B.4, Fig. 6(b)","excerpt":"the MARL-meta solution consistently delivers latency performance closest to the optimal LDTP solution, outperforming other traditional MARL approaches.","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Under dynamic UAV count changes (5 to 8 to 6 UAVs), MARL-meta adapts more efficiently with higher rewards and lower latency than MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC.","page":12,"locator":"VI-B.5, Fig. 7(a)-(b)","excerpt":"As the number of UAVs fluctuates, MARL-meta adapts more efficiently, achieving the highest rewards by quickly reallocating resources","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Under dynamic UAV memory changes (560 MB to 256 MB to 512 MB with 5 UAVs), MARL-meta adapts faster and achieves lower latency than traditional DRL approaches.","page":12,"locator":"VI-B.5, Fig. 7(c)","excerpt":"Throughout these dynamic memory changes, the MARL-meta algorithm outperforms traditional DRL approaches, demonstrating superior adaptability","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"As the number of UAVs increases (5, 7, 9 UAVs), MARL-meta stays closer to LDTP and outperforms MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC in average total latency and minimum transmit power.","page":13,"locator":"VI-B.6, Fig. 8(a)-(b)","excerpt":"our proposed MARL-meta solution provides results closer to the LDTP solution and outperforms the traditional DRL-based solutions, achieving better results across all cases of UAV numbers.","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"As the covered area size increases (36, 49, 64 cells), MARL-meta stays closer to LDTP and outperforms MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC in average total latency and minimum transmit power.","page":13,"locator":"VI-B.7, Fig. 9(a)-(b)","excerpt":"our proposed MARL-meta solution provides results closer to the LDTP solution and outperforms the traditional DRL-based solutions, achieving better results across all cases of area sizes.","quantitative":null}],"withheld":[{"topic":"~29% latency improvement over competitive methods","reason":"only sourced by abstract (page 1)","checked":"physical pages 10-13, Section VI and Section VII (Conclusion)"},{"topic":"~23% transmission power improvement over competitive methods","reason":"only sourced by abstract (page 1)","checked":"physical pages 10-13, Section VI and Section VII (Conclusion)"},{"topic":"~9% latency comparability relative to traditional LDTP optimization solution","reason":"only sourced by abstract (page 1)","checked":"physical pages 10-13, Section VI and Section VII (Conclusion)"}]}
{"key":"K02","report_id":"R0074","field":"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability","assertions":[{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"At the Llama-2 70B scale (64-token input, 1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s air-to-ground), Edge-Solo and cloud-edge baselines (Cloud-Edge-Even, Cloud-Edge-Opt) failed with out-of-memory (OOM) errors.","page":16,"locator":"Section 4.2, Fig. 4","excerpt":"At the massive 70B scale, cloud-edge baselines entirely failed due to memory constraints, underscoring the inherent limitations of existing offloading approaches.","quantitative":null},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"At the Llama-2 70B scale in the same scenario, distributed collaborative frameworks Orion, Jupiter, and EdgeShard all succeeded, with Orion achieving the lowest latency among the successful distributed solutions.","page":17,"locator":"Section 4.2, Fig. 4","excerpt":"Among all successful distributed solutions, Orion remained the most efficient","quantitative":null}],"withheld":[{"topic":"Exclusivity claim that Orion is the only framework capable of running the full 70B model on memory-constrained UAV nodes","reason":"contradicted by Section 4.2 (pages 16-17) and Figure 4, which show Jupiter and EdgeShard also successfully executed Llama-2 70B","checked":"physical pages 16-17, Section 4.2 and Figure 4"}]}
{"key":"K03","report_id":"R0121","field":"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability","assertions":[{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"L2 encapsulation (SwarmNS-MAC) reduces inter-node traffic volume by approximately 3.7% compared to the L2-L7 protocol stack.","page":6,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph 2","excerpt":"traffic volume is reduced by ≈3.7%","quantitative":{"value":"≈3.7","units":"% reduction","baseline":"L2-L7 protocol stack (94 B header overhead, 1448 B payload)","denominator":"L2-L7 stack traffic volume","workload":"256×80×80 intermediate activation tensor (1,638,400 elements)","conditions":"single-hop transmission at R = 12 Mbit/s"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"L2 encapsulation (SwarmNS-MAC) reduces transmission time by approximately 7.9% compared to the L2-L7 protocol stack.","page":6,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph 2","excerpt":"transmission time by ≈7.9%","quantitative":{"value":"≈7.9","units":"% reduction","baseline":"L2-L7 protocol stack (94 B header overhead, 1448 B payload)","denominator":"L2-L7 stack transmission time","workload":"256×80×80 intermediate activation tensor (1,638,400 elements)","conditions":"single-hop transmission at R = 12 Mbit/s"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"L2 encapsulation (SwarmNS-MAC) reduces the number of transmitted frames by approximately 36.5% compared to the L2-L7 protocol stack.","page":6,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph 2","excerpt":"the number of frames is reduced by ≈36.5%","quantitative":{"value":"≈36.5","units":"% reduction","baseline":"L2-L7 protocol stack (1448 B payload)","denominator":"L2-L7 stack frame count","workload":"256×80×80 intermediate activation tensor (1,638,400 elements)","conditions":"single-hop transmission at R = 12 Mbit/s"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Post-training quantization from FP32 to 0.8 bits/element reduces per-hop transmission time approximately 40-fold (from 4.9 s to 122.8 ms) at 12 Mbit/s.","page":6,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph 2, Fig. 2","excerpt":"reduces transmission time by approximately 40 times - from 4.9 s to 122.8 ms per hop at a rate of 12 Mbit/s","quantitative":{"value":"40","units":"times reduction (4.9 s to 122.8 ms)","baseline":"FP32 (32 bits/element, 4911 ms per hop)","denominator":"FP32 per-hop transmission time","workload":"256×80×80 intermediate activation tensor","conditions":"L2 encapsulation, single hop, R = 12 Mbit/s, post-training compression at 0.8 bits/element"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"For FP32 (32 bits), SwarmNS-MAC L2 encapsulation transmits in 4911 ms (2875 frames) versus 5332 ms (4526 frames) for L2-L7.","page":7,"locator":"Table 3, row FP32 (32 bits), columns Frames L2/L7 and T_tx L2/L7","excerpt":"FP32 (32 bits) 6553.6 2875 4526 4911 5332","quantitative":{"value":"4911 vs 5332","units":"ms","baseline":"L2-L7 stack (5332 ms, 4526 frames)","denominator":"single-hop tensor transmission","workload":"256×80×80 tensor (S_a = 6553.6 kB) at FP32 (32 bits)","conditions":"single hop, R = 12 Mbit/s, L2 (2875 frames) vs L7 (4526 frames)"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"For INT8 (8 bits), SwarmNS-MAC L2 encapsulation transmits in 1228 ms (719 frames) versus 1333 ms (1132 frames) for L2-L7.","page":7,"locator":"Table 3, row INT8 (8 bits), columns Frames L2/L7 and T_tx L2/L7","excerpt":"INT8 (8 bits) 1638.4 719 1132 1228 1333","quantitative":{"value":"1228 vs 1333","units":"ms","baseline":"L2-L7 stack (1333 ms, 1132 frames)","denominator":"single-hop tensor transmission","workload":"256×80×80 tensor (S_a = 1638.4 kB) at INT8 (8 bits)","conditions":"single hop, R = 12 Mbit/s, L2 (719 frames) vs L7 (1132 frames)"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"For ADT (0.8 bits), SwarmNS-MAC L2 encapsulation transmits in 122.8 ms (72 frames) versus 133.5 ms (114 frames) for L2-L7.","page":7,"locator":"Table 3, row ADT (0.8 bits) [16], columns Frames L2/L7 and T_tx L2/L7","excerpt":"ADT (0.8 bits) [16] 163.8 72 114 122.8 133.5","quantitative":{"value":"122.8 vs 133.5","units":"ms","baseline":"L2-L7 stack (133.5 ms, 114 frames)","denominator":"single-hop tensor transmission","workload":"256×80×80 tensor (S_a = 163.8 kB) at ADT (0.8 bits)","conditions":"single hop, R = 12 Mbit/s, L2 (72 frames) vs L7 (114 frames)"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"For an INT8 tensor at per-frame success probability p_f = 0.9995, timely intact tensor delivery probability is 0.70 for SwarmNS-MAC (719 frames) versus 0.57 for L2-L7 (1,132 frames).","page":7,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph below Fig. 2","excerpt":"the probability of timely delivery of the complete tensor is 0.70 for SwarmNS-MAC (719 frames) versus 0.57 for the L2-L7 stack (1,132 frames).","quantitative":{"value":"0.70 vs 0.57","units":"probability","baseline":"L2-L7 stack (0.57 at 1,132 frames)","denominator":"complete tensor delivery attempts","workload":"INT8 256×80×80 tensor","conditions":"per-frame transmission success probability p_f = 0.9995"}},{"status":"OBSERVED","finding":"With n = 6 neighbors and timely tensor delivery probability p_d = 0.70, aggregation quorum probability is 0.93 for q = 3 versus 0.74 for q = 4.","page":7,"locator":"Section 6, paragraph below Fig. 2, Fig. 3","excerpt":"for n = 6 neighbors and q = 3, we have P_q = 0.93 versus 0.74 for q = 4 when p_d = 0.70","quantitative":{"value":"0.93 vs 0.74","units":"probability","baseline":"MinQuorum q = 4 (0.74)","denominator":"n = 6 neighbors","workload":"INT8 tensor aggregation","conditions":"timely tensor delivery probability p_d = 0.70 (SwarmNS-MAC, p_f = 0.9995)"}}],"withheld":[]}
